==============================================================
Full-system binary-only instrumentation in Renode for afl-fuzz
==============================================================

  (See ../README.md for the general instruction manual.)

0) TL;DR: just run anything
---------------------------

1. Download latest portable build of Renode from https://builds.renode.io/
   and unpack it to ~/renode or anywhere else.

2. Build some system to test, e.g. Zephyr shell sample for nRF52840 and
   copy it to this renode_mode directory (you will need Zephyr SDK, see
   their docs[0]):

   $ west build -b nrf52840dk_nrf52840 samples/subsys/shell/shell_module
   $ cp build/zephyr/zephyr.elf /path/to/AFL/renode_mode

[0]: https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/develop/getting_started/

3. (If needed, edit the example.resc file; by default nRF52840 is used)
4. (If needed, edit the afl_uart.py file; serial device might be named
    differently on other boards/targets)
5. (If needed, adjust any other constants in afl_renode.py)
6. Run:

   $ cd /path/to/AFL
   $ make
   $ mkdir INPUTS
   $ echo 'abcdefxyz' > INPUTS/some-testcase  # true corpus works better
   $ PATH=$PATH:<path-to-renode> AFL_SKIP_BIN_CHECK=1 ./afl-fuzz -t 15000 -i INPUTS -o OUTPUTS -R -- renode_mode/example.resc

Notes:

    On Cortex-M, jumping to a NULL function pointer raises a UsageFault
    instead of executing at address 0, so besides PC 0 the harness also
    treats entering ``k_sys_fatal_error_handler`` (Zephyr) as a crash.
    Override the crash symbols with a comma-separated list in
    ``AFL_RENODE_CRASH_SYMBOLS``.

    If ``core_pattern`` pipes to a crash handler (e.g. systemd-coredump)
    and you cannot change it, set ``AFL_I_DONT_CARE_ABOUT_MISSING_CRASHES=1``;
    crashes are reported by the harness, not by real core dumps.

    The ``efr32mg_sltb004a`` Zephyr board is now named ``sltb004a``.

    While ``renode_mode/example.resc`` is executable, it shouldn't be run as a separate, standalone Renode script - it depends on several settings provided by ``afl-renode-trace``.

1) Introduction
---------------

Renode[1] is an open-source simulation framework developed by Antmicro,
allowing users to run their unmodified software on a range of embedded
targets, ranging from MCUs to multi-core Linux-capable systems, on
various architectures, like ARM Cortex-A,R,M, RISC-V, POWER and others.
Renode allows for single- and multi-node simulation via various
connectivity methods: Ethernet, BLE, IEEE802.15.4 etc.

[1]: https://www.renode.io

The code in this directory is used for a standalone feature that
leverages Renode and allows callers to obtain instrumentation output for
black-box, closed-source binaries for full bare-metal systems rather
than user-mode executables.  This mechanism can be then used by afl-fuzz
to stress-test targets in a context of a full system simulation,
including the OS and HAL level, or targets that couldn't be built as
user-mode binaries.

The idea is heavily based on deferred fork server mode and QEMU mode.

2) How to use
-------------

The feature is implemented with a fairly small plugin for Renode.  As
usual with Renode, you need a Renode script and platform description.
See its documentation[2] for more details about that.  The default
harness assumes a single-node setup instrumenting code executed on
sysbus.cpu and feeding input through sysbus.uart0 char-by-char whenever
the CPU looks idle.

[2]: https://renode.readthedocs.io/

The example script ready for fuzzing is in the example.resc file.  It is
based on the nRF52840 platform, but can be easily adapted to other
Renode-supported targets.

If you want to change the harness, you can do so by copying the
afl_uart.py file over, changing it, and importing the new file instead
in your .resc file of choice, 'import antigravity'-style.  See the
example.resc file for how to do it.

3) Zephyr syscall harness
-------------------------

afl_syscall.py and example-syscall.resc fuzz Zephyr system calls (k_poll
and friends) from a user mode thread on cortex_r8_virtual.  The guest
application is in zephyr-syscall-fuzz/; build it with:

  west build -b cortex_r8_virtual renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz

Each test case is written into the guest's afl_input buffer right after
a machine reset (afl_renode.always_reset), and a run ends when the guest
calls afl_done (success), afl_bug (kernel fault/panic/assert, reported
as SIGABRT) or afl_hang, or when the host watchdog expires after
AFL_RENODE_MAX_QUANTA quanta.  User thread oopses are expected and are
not crashes.  An optional second argument to afl-renode-trace selects
the ELF:

  afl-fuzz -t 15000 -i renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/seeds -o out \
      -R -- renode_mode/example-syscall.resc zephyr-syscall-fuzz.elf

Building with CONFIG_SYSCALL_FUZZ_SELFTEST=y plants kernel bugs (input
bytes 0xF0/0xF1/0xF2: panic, fault, assertion) to check that crash
detection works.

The guest build has no console output, for speed. Before reporting a
failure the guest fills an afl_crash record (fatal reason, thread,
exception frame, DFSR/DFAR/IFSR/IFAR, assertion file:line, and the input
offset of the op being executed). With AFL_RENODE_CRASH_INFO=<dir> the
harness decodes it into <dir>/<sha1 of input>.txt, so each file in
crashes/ can be matched with `sha1sum`. To look at a single input:

  renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/replay.sh zephyr-syscall-fuzz.elf <input>

prints the same summary, and with `-g 3333` keeps the crashed machine
alive behind a GDB server (`-w` waits for the debugger before booting).
Replay on the same ELF that found the crash: inputs may contain raw
addresses that are only meaningful for that build.

Coverage is selected with AFL_RENODE_COVERAGE:

  window  (default) AFL-style edge coverage, only between user_entry and
          the afl_* marker that ends the run; boot and setup are the same
          for every input and would only add noise
  kernel  like window, but without the harness's own functions, listed
          in <ELF>.cov (written at build time by cov_ranges.py)
  all     the original afl_renode block coverage, from reset

In 5 minute side-by-side runs, window reached 2745 exact kernel edges,
all 1521 and kernel 1216: the harness's own coverage tells AFL which op
sequences are new, and is a useful stepping stone. To compare corpora on
exact kernel edges, replay them through one Renode instance:

  AFL_RENODE_COVERAGE=kernel renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/replay_corpus.py \
      -e edges.txt renode_mode/example-syscall.resc zephyr-syscall-fuzz.elf \
      out/default/queue/id*
  wc -l edges.txt

Do not switch the block hook on and off from cpu.AddHook callbacks: the
change takes effect at nondeterministic points, which dropped stability
to ~77% and produced false hangs. afl_syscall keeps the hook installed
and gates coverage in Python instead, at no measurable cost.
